## Slide 5

Task Definition and Task Forms 

Task Definitions
When configured without a Task Form, all properties and assignments can be made directly to a Task Definition. However, to simply repeated configuration, Task Forms should be used.
Task Forms
A Task Form is a non-system specific entity that can be assigned to a Task Definition. It defines a set of common inputs and can be repeated across multiple Task Definitions. 
Trigger Type
When using Task Forms, a configurable Trigger Type can be assigned. This includes triggering based on Tags, Schedules, and various Data hooks. Additional properties will appear based on the Type selected.

## Slide 6

Task Form Properties 

Versioning
Task Forms can be versioned. The creation and management of the version is controlled in the configuration screens through dedicated menu options.
Task Completion Seconds
Drives logic that determines when a Task is considered late (optional).  This can drive scripted business rules and Notifications.

## Slide 7

Task Form Properties 

Versioning
Task Forms can be versioned. The creation and management of the version is controlled in the configuration screens through dedicated menu options.
Task Completion Seconds
Drives logic that determines when a Task is considered late (optional).  This can drive scripted business rules and Notifications.

## Slide 8

Task Form Items 

Represents configurable data entry fields to be collected during completion of a specific Task
Task Form Items are organized into groups called Task Form Item Groups
One or more Task Form Item Groups are assigned to a Task Definition defining the fields to be captured in the UI Form

Task Forms in the TS Web UI render the defined Task Form Items as form input fields…
Text Boxes
Dropdown Lists
Check Boxes
Support basic validation (min, max, required)

Configuration 

Execution

